  "summary": "Greentext, ASCII art and anonymity: the web's most influential subculture engine.",
  "description": "Imageboard culture — 2channel's Japanese anonymous BBS tradition (1999) and 4chan's image-board adaptation (2003) — created the internet's most productive visual-language lab: greentext storytelling (\">be me\" narrative dialect), ASCII/Shift_JIS art (Mona), reaction-image repertoires, character memes (Pedobear, longcat, Pepe's origin), and anonymous collective authorship that spawns and kills memes in days. Its aesthetics — compressed JPEGs, saved-and-resaved decay, tripcodes, \"[s4s]\" meta-irony — are vernacular modernism of the anonymous web. 4chan alone birthed Anonymous, lolcats, rage comics and Wojak; the culture's dual legacy (creative engine + toxicity engine) makes it the internet's most studied folk space.",
  "culturalContext": "Imageboard culture is the anonymous web's creative engine: greentext, ASCII art and reaction-image repertoires birthed Anonymous, lolcats and Wojak — and the culture's dual legacy (invention and toxicity) makes it the internet's most studied folk space, essential reading for understanding meme evolution.",
